.TH SIGNER 8
.SH NAME
signer, verify, countersigner \- set-top box authentication
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B auth/signer
.PP
.BI auth/verify " set-top-box-id"
.PP
.B auth/countersigner
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Signer
and
.I countersigner
answer requests on the service ports
.B infsigner
and
.BR infcsigner ,
respectively, on a machine acting as authentication server for a network.
They are not started by
.IR svc (8):
no program in this system is their client (the
.B register
command of the set-top boxes they were written for is not here), they answer anybody who connects, and
the certificates they issue have no expiry time.
A realm that wants this enrolment starts them on purpose, after
.B svc/auth
(which starts
.IR keyfs (4)):
.IP
.EX
listen -v -t -A 'tcp!*!infsigner' {auth/signer&}
listen -v -t -A 'tcp!*!infcsigner' {auth/countersigner&}
.EE
.PP
or, from the shell functions of
.BR /lib/sh/srv ,
.BR srv.signer .
.I Verify
is invoked on the same server, after
.I signer
but before
.IR countersigner ,
following an independent check of a caller's credentials.
.PP
.I Signer
constructs an authentication certificate from the signer's key (in
.BR /keydb/signerkey )
and information from the requesting client, including
the set top box ID.
The signer's key is made by
.IR createsignerkey (8).
With no key file, or one that does not read as a key or has expired,
.I signer
signs nothing, tells the caller the service is unavailable,
and says on its standard error to run
.IR createsignerkey .
(It once made a key itself, owned by
.LR * ,
in place of whatever file did not read:
a damaged key file was silently replaced, orphaning every certificate of the realm.)
In CNSA mode
.RB ( /env/cnsamode
set) the key must be CNSA's whole, as
.B "createsignerkey -c"
makes it, or nothing is signed.
.PP
The set top box ID is the owner's name in the key the client sends.
It becomes the name of a file in
.B /keydb/signed
and the name certified, so
.I signer
takes only one account name as
.IR login (6)
defines it (no
.B /
or
.BR \e ,
not
.B .
or
.BR .. ,
no control character), and refuses any other before it signs or writes anything.
It does not certify its own name (the owner of
.BR /keydb/signerkey )
for anybody, nor the name
.LR * .
The key certified must be of the signer's own algorithm,
and an RSA, ElGamal or DSA key must have a modulus of 2048 bits or more.
.PP
A request is made before anything is known of its caller, so what one
caller can cost is bounded.
One request waits for a name at a time:
a second for that name is refused while the first waits for
.I verify
(it once replaced the first, and the operator then compared the wrong caller's digest).
A request not answered by the operator within 15 minutes is discarded.
At most 256 requests wait.
Requests are counted by the throttle of
.IR logind (8)
(in
.BR /keydb/loginfail ):
after ten in a row the next are refused for a minute.
A caller that is refused is sent an error message saying why
(the protocol's
.BR ! InnnP
message, which
.B Keyring->getmsg
gives as the error string); a refusal was once a connection that closed.
A certificate that would not fit one of the protocol's messages
.RB ( Keyring->Maxmsg ,
which holds the largest: an ML-DSA-87 or SLH-DSA-256s one)
is refused before any record of the request is made.
.PP
.I Signer
`blinds'
the certificate by XOR-ing it with a random bit mask, then sends the result to the requesting client.
The client machine's user uses that information to establish identity with a human agent on the
signing machine.
.I Signer
also saves the both the `blinded' and `unblinded' result from the input in
.BI /keydb/signed/ set-top-box-id
for
.I verify
(see below).
.PP
.I Verify
is run on the signing server
by the agency running the authentication server, in response to
a call from a remote user who has run a client of
.I signer
(the set-top boxes'
.B register
command, which is not in this system, or an equivalent).
.I Verify
checks a caller's identity using information from the file
.BI /keydb/signed/ set-top-box-id
created by
.IR signer .
The file contains the previously crafted authentication certificate and the `blinded' version of the certificate that was sent to the requesting client.
.PP
.I Verify
displays a digest of the `blinded' version, as 32 words, so that it can be compared to that reported by the set-top-box owner over a secure independent mechanism (for example, telephone).
The digest is the first 16 bytes of the SHA-256 of the signer's public key, the caller's, and the blinded certificate
(it was an MD5).
A request the operator answers is removed from
.BR /keydb/signed ,
accepted or rejected, so that a rejected caller can ask again.
If the operator of
.I verify
is convinced of the identity of the caller, the operator should accept when prompted by
.IR verify .
.I Verify
then writes the authentication certificate to
.BI /keydb/countersigned/ set-top-box-id,
as input for
.I countersigner
(see
.IR signer (8)).
.PP
.I Note:
if the operator of
.I verify
accepts the identity, the set-top-box owner should be requested
to answer `yes' to the prompt displayed by that client.
The order of
acceptance\-first on the signer, then on the client\-is essential,
to produce the countersigned certificate before invoking
.I countersigner
to read it.
.PP
.I Countersigner
sends the blinding data in
.BI /keydb/countersigned/ set-top-box-id
to the requesting client.
The ID a client gives must again be one name;
a client whose ID is no name, or names no approved request, is sent an
error message saying which; and
.I countersigner
runs in a name space in which
.B /keydb
holds only the contents of
.BR /keydb/countersigned ,
so that the records an operator has approved are all an ID can name.
(An ID of
.B ../signed/\fIid\fP
was answered with the blinding data of a request nobody had approved,
which with the blinded certificate
.I signer
had sent is a certificate.)
.SH FILES
.TF /keydb/countersigned/set-top-box-id
.TP
.B /keydb/signerkey
Secret key of the `signer' host.
.TP
.BI /keydb/signed/ set-top-box-id
Repository of `blinded' and clear certificates.
.TP
.BI /keydb/countersigned/ set-top-box-id
Repository of `unblinded' certificates.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/auth/signer.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/auth/verify.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/auth/countersigner.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR login (6),
.IR createsignerkey (8),
.IR svc (8)
